+// The backup is worth keeping only while it holds something no file has.
+//
+// Orca's backup directory is what the "Previously unsaved items have been
+// detected. Do you want to restore them?" offer reads on the next launch: it
+// asks whether <backup>/.3mf is there and whether the process named in the
+// lock.txt beside it is gone. On a desktop both halves are true only after a
+// crash. On iOS the second half is true after *every* session - there is no
+// quit on this platform, the system kills the process - so once 0424 made that
+// test answerable at all, the offer came up on every launch, on top of a home
+// page whose tiles were already showing the very work it was offering to
+// rescue. 0424 was right that iOS is the platform that needs crash recovery
+// most; what it did not do was give the recovery any way to tell that the kill
+// had cost nothing.
+//
+// Taking the offer is also what put the project on the home page twice.
+// A restore reinstates the project's identity from origin.txt, and origin.txt
+// is written once - when the file was first loaded - and never again. A project
+// that has moved since (saved to Documents/X.3mf, or written to the copy under
+// Documents/Autosave while it had no file of its own) came back under the path
+// it was opened from, beside the tile for the path it actually lives at. Two
+// tiles, one name, one project.
+//
+// So the moment the autosave has put this work into a file, the backup copy of
+// the same work has nothing left to offer and it goes. This is precisely what
+// save_project() does through Slic3r::remove_backup(model, false) after an
+// explicit save - and on this port the autosave *is* the save, so it has to
+// keep the same promise. Done directly rather than through the backup manager
+// because that queues the removal onto its own thread, and the call that
+// matters most is the one from willResignActive, where there may be no next
+// moment in which that thread is scheduled.
+//
+// What this leaves is the invariant the offer needs in order to be honest:
+// <backup>/.3mf exists only while there are changes that no file has. Nothing
+// is given up by that - the backup timer writes it again as soon as the model
+// moves on, so a crash between two autosaves still has a copy at most ten
+// seconds old to come back from, which is the case the offer is actually for.
+//
+// origin.txt is rewritten rather than removed. It is the identity a restore
+// would hand back, and it is also the archive the importer falls back to for
+// anything missing from the backup, so pointing it at the file that now holds
+// this project is both the answer that stops a restore landing beside its own
+// tile and the more complete of the two files.
+static void orca_ios_backup_overtaken_by(Plater *plater, const boost::filesystem::path &saved)
+{
+    const std::string backup = plater->model().current_backup_path();
+    if (backup.empty())
+        return;
+
+    {
+        std::ofstream origin(backup + "/origin.txt",
+                             std::ios::out | std::ios::binary | std::ios::trunc);
+        origin << saved.string();
+    }
+
+    boost::system::error_code ec;
+    const boost::filesystem::path stale = boost::filesystem::path(backup) / ".3mf";
+    if (!boost::filesystem::exists(stale, ec))
+        return;
+    boost::filesystem::remove(stale, ec);
+    BOOST_LOG_TRIVIAL(error) << "orca-ios-autosave: retired the backup " << stale.string()
+                             << (ec ? " FAILED: " + ec.message() : std::string(" ok"))
+                             << ", origin is now " << saved.string();
+}
